messages:
Marta S: Opened a docs PR to standardize an explicit envelope for returned arrays to avoid ambiguous parser behavior. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/509. Asking for two small SDK normalization snippets (Go + Ruby) and a one-line API note for the reference. Need wording for the migration note.

Ben L: curious  why pick `entries` over `items`? we've used `items` a lot in examples.

Marta S: `items` collides with internal API name in several tool callers (it gets interpreted as an instruction to iterate). `entries` is slightly more explicit and less likely to be overloaded. proposal includes `envelope_type` as discriminator: `{ "envelope_type": "list", "entries": [...] }` so parsers can short-circuit correctly.

Noor A: ok, makes sense. Can someone add a short JSON Schema snippet that shows the preferred envelope vs raw-array, and a validator rule example? small note about union element types would be nice (string | object).

Carlos V: I'll drop the schema. Example to use in PR: 

```json
// preferred envelope
{
  "envelope_type": "list",
  "entries": [
    { "kind": "text", "text": "hello" },
    "plain string entry"
  ]
}

// ambiguous top-level array
[
  { "kind": "text", "text": "hello" },
  "plain string entry"
]

// JSON Schema (simplified)
{
  "oneOf": [
    { "type": "object", "properties": { "envelope_type": { "const": "list" } } },
    { "type": "array" }
  ]
}
```

Noor A: for migration guide: add a short code snippet per SDK that normalizes before handing to the parser. I'll draft the Ruby sample.

Noor A: Ruby snippet for the docs: 

```ruby
def normalize_response(res)
  if res.is_a?(Array)
    { "envelope_type" => "list", "entries" => res }
  else
    res
  end
end
```

Marta S: perfect. Can we also include a note in the API reference: "To avoid ambiguous parsing of top-level arrays, prefer returning an envelope with `envelope_type` and `entries`." Keep it short.

Ben L: I'll add a Go helper for examples.

Ben L: Go snippet: 

```go
func NormalizeResponse(res interface{}) map[string]interface{} {
    switch v := res.(type) {
    case []interface{}:
        return map[string]interface{}{"envelope_type": "list", "entries": v}
    default:
        if m, ok := v.(map[string]interface{}); ok {
            return m
        }
        return map[string]interface{}{"value": v}
    }
}
```

Carlos V: can we add a short section about union element types: recommend wrapping primitive strings into objects like `{ "kind": "text", "text": "..." }` so downstream routing is unambiguous.

Marta S: yes. Also adding a tiny eval test: prompt that sometimes returns `["x", {"kind":"image","url":"..."}]` and a regression check ensuring the normalized envelope is produced.

